Kepler, an open-source agent harness for ARC-AGI-3

This project adapts, with gratitude and attribution, design ideas from:

- The executable-world-model approach on ARC-AGI-3 was established in published
  work by multiple groups (Tycho, arXiv:2607.28287; Rodionov/baseline1,
  arXiv:2605.05138); this project is an independent implementation of that
  idea: have the model build
  an executable world model of the game rather than play it.

- Retrodict (Ryan Brown), sparse expected-cell predictions, the escalation ladder
  (300-action / 2-reset tiers, escalation-only anti-pruning text), prediction
  discipline and search-cost prose, objects() segmentation.
  https://github.com/ryanbbrown/Retrodict

- Tycho (NIMI-research; Aioanei, Lehmann, Vahdati et al., arXiv:2607.28287) -
  partial (UNKNOWN-cell) predictions and vacuous-coverage rejection, threaded
  hidden-state planning, the 5x-baseline level cutoff, the consecutive-RESET
  zero-cost guard, the hedged no-op message, and the Brahe/Kepler framing this
  project's name completes. Apache-2.0.
  https://github.com/NIMI-research/Tycho

- ARC Prize Foundation, ARC-AGI-3, the arc-agi toolkit and the official RHAE
  scorecard implementation. https://arcprize.org

Kepler independently implements the credited research ideas. No third-party
source code is copied into this repository unless a file says so explicitly.
Research attribution is not a claim that every cited repository grants a reuse
license.
